    {
      "@id": "skills/tools/folio-core/skills-and-tools.md#provenance-1",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Skills and Tools — the skill says what, the Tool says how",
      "prov:value": "Decided 2026-09-18 by the repository owner. Schema for the Tool node:\n`docs/architecture/cat-harness-minimum.md` §\"Strawperson\"; carrier\nconvention (Zod authoritative, JSON-LD and JSON Schema generated) in\n[`directory-conventions`](../../kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md) §\"What lives in the\n`schemas` graph\".",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-18"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-18"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/tools/folio-core/skills-and-tools.md#provenance-2",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "The test, in one line",
      "prov:value": "**When the mechanism is itself a procedure**, it is the Tool's subprocess\n(owner, 2026-09-30: *\"tools can describe their own specific subprocesses if\nneeded to not bog down general skills\"*). The Tool node names the BPMN in\n`subprocesses` (process ids, checked by `check:tools`), conventionally under\n`processes/tools/`; the skill keeps the portable sentence. See\n[`bpmn-processes`](../../process/workflow/bpmn-processes.md).",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-30"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-30"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/tools/folio-core/skills-and-tools.md#provenance-3",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "date",
      "cat-harness:section": "Every bean skill has (at least) two Tools",
      "prov:value": "Both satisfy the same skills. Neither is the skill. A fresh container has no\n`beans` on `PATH`, so an agent that only knows the CLI is an agent that reads\nthe plan and touches nothing — which is exactly the 2026-09-18 failure where a\nsession completed two merged PRs' worth of durable work **unclaimed**. The\nfallback is not a degraded mode to mention in passing; it is a second Tool with\nequal standing, and the skill must present it that way.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-18"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-18"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/tools/folio-core/skills-and-tools.md#provenance-4",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Every bean skill has (at least) two Tools",
      "prov:value": "**An external project is a candidate Tool before it is anything else.** When\nsomebody asks whether another project could do a skill's job — Beads for the\nwork plan, say — that survey is research, not a proposal and not a skill edit:\nit files under `<instance>/docs/research-and-analysis/` per\n[`placement`](../../kg/kg-core/placement.md), dated, with each project's health measured rather\nthan recalled. The first one is `research-and-analysis/prior-art.md`\n(2026-09-23), and the choice it raised is a bean (`b91x`), not a paragraph\nhere. Only if a candidate is adopted does it become a Tool node, with the\n`selection` its derived alternatives call for, below.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean b91x",
        "2026-09-23"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-23"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/tools/folio-core/skills-and-tools.md#provenance-5",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "issue",
      "cat-harness:section": "Substitutable Tools are derived, and each says how to choose",
      "prov:value": "A pair like that is **substitutable**: same job, different mechanism, pick one.\nNobody declares it. Two Tools are alternatives when they **satisfy a common\nskill AND have the same I/O signature** — the same input names, types and\nrequired flags, the same outputs, the same `renders` and `maintains`\n(`deriveAlternatives` in `schemas/tools/tool.ts`; #1168, B9a). A shared skill alone\nis not enough: most skills with several Tools have complementary ones, steps\nrather than choices, and their I/O is what tells them apart.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "#1168"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/tools/folio-core/skills-and-tools.md#provenance-6",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "issue",
      "cat-harness:section": "The harness assumes no MCP — and knows how to emit one",
      "prov:value": "**Editing a skill goes through `skill_update`, not a raw file write**\n(folio-assistant#875). Where the server runs, it is the write side of\n`skill_fetch`. It refuses unless every gate passes:",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "folio-assistant#875"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/tools/folio-core/skills-and-tools.md#provenance-7",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "pr",
      "cat-harness:section": "GitHub is a Tool node, not a layer",
      "prov:value": "**Measured before deciding, on `main` 2026-09-18.** `coordinate.md` is 731\nlines with 94 matches for GitHub-ish terms — but only **12** of those are\nconcrete invocations (`mcp__github__*`, `gh pr`, `gh api`). The other 82 are\nconceptual: \"pull request\", \"PR\", \"GitHub\" as a noun. Across all four skills:\n1,404 lines, of which the genuinely forge-bound mechanism is on the order of a\nfew dozen lines.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-18"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-18"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/tools/folio-core/skills-and-tools.md#provenance-8",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "The forge operations, one Tool node each",
      "prov:value": "Owner, 2026-10-20 (bean `folio-assistant-4dbr`, option A): **one Tool node per\nforge operation**, using the fields Tools already have. A skill names the\n*operation* in neutral words, and this table is where an operation meets its\nnodes. An agent picks the node for the forge the checkout's remote is on; each\npair is a derived alternative carrying `selection` (`check:tools` holds it).",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean 4dbr",
        "2026-10-20"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-20"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/tools/folio-core/skills-and-tools.md#provenance-9",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "The forge operations, one Tool node each",
      "prov:value": "A dash is a gap, not an oversight; each is recorded on the bean. Two\nGitLab nodes run a script rather than `glab` directly (owner, 2026-10-24,\n\"Fill both gaps\"): `glab mr diff` has no `--name-only`, so `gitlab-mr-files`\nreads the paths out of the raw diff; and `glab mr update --reviewer NAME`\nreplaces the reviewers, so `gitlab-mr-request-review` passes `+NAME`, which\nadds one. Its names are `GitLabUsername`s, since GitLab usernames do not fit\n`Login`.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-10-24"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-24"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/tools/folio-core/skills-and-tools.md#provenance-10",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "A Tool may describe its own subprocess",
      "prov:value": "Owner, 2026-09-30: *\"in general tools can describe their own specific\nsubprocesses if needed to not bog down general skills\"*. When one mechanism\ntakes several steps, they belong to **the Tool**, not to the general skill: the\nTool lists `subprocesses` (process ids, each the stem of a `.bpmn` the checkout declares),\nand a general diagram's call activity is bound by it. **General skills name the\nTool, never its steps.** First case: `render-kg-to-cdn` is the general step\n(render a Knowledge Graph for a CDN at a publication root URL; output a status\nand a message), and `gh-pages` is one Tool for it whose subprocess is\nbootstrap-tools' `render-kg-to-github-pages.bpmn` — declared below the harness,\nso the reference points down.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-30"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-30"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/tools/folio-core/skills-and-tools.md#provenance-11",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Where this stands — the schema is real, the migration is half done",
      "prov:value": "### How that migration was done, because the method matters more than the result"
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/tools/folio-core/skills-and-tools.md#provenance-12",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "issue",
      "cat-harness:section": "A `satisfies` edge is checkable against the skill's own contract",
      "prov:value": "**`satisfies` is the one part of a Tool node a schema cannot check, and it was\nbeing written on judgement alone.** `bun run cat check:tools` compares a Tool's\n`io` against the input contract of every skill it claims — the contract the\nskill names in its own front matter (`input:`), never one found by directory\nname (#1168). Two rules: **every `required` property of the contract must\nappear as an input on the Tool**, and **where a Tool input and a contract\nproperty share a name, their JSON types must agree** (an `integer` port may\nserve a `number` property). If either fails, the Tool cannot exercise the skill\nand the edge is false.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "#1168"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/tools/folio-core/skills-and-tools.md#provenance-13",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "A `satisfies` edge is checkable against the skill's own contract",
      "prov:value": "**It caught two edges on its first run, both written the previous day and both\nwrong.** `translation-validate` claimed `content-validate`, whose contract\nrequires `targetPath` — validating a `.po` against its `.pot` is not validating\na folio's content. `workflow-complete` claimed `dmn-authoring`, whose contract\nrequires `decisionName` and `inputVariables` — what you supply to *write* a\ndecision table, not to *answer* one. Both edges are dropped, which is why\ncoverage reads 24 rather than 26: the earlier number counted two skills as\ncovered by Tools that could not perform them."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/tools/folio-core/skills-and-tools.md#provenance-14",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "date",
      "cat-harness:section": "Which uncovered skills actually need one — `bun run cat tools:coverage`",
      "prov:value": "**Run it for the numbers; do not read them here.** The figures that stood in this\nparagraph until 2026-09-20 — 16 / 3 / 52 / 47 over 118 uncovered — had drifted to\n28 / 4 / 53 / 59 over 144 without anybody noticing, because a count in prose is a\nclaim that nothing re-checks. The tiers are the durable part; the tallies belong\nto whichever run you are looking at.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-20"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-20"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
